on unavailable this session (noted explicitly, per constraints).\n\n### 6. Recommendation\n\n**6a.** Use `SPACE_UNIT` (name via `SPACE_UNIT`, join key `SPACE_UNIT_CODE`) ⋈ `ZPM_ROOMS_LOAD` (room rows, `ACCESS_LEVEL`). Measure: `COUNT(*)` = rooms assigned; conditional count `SUM(ACCESS_LEVEL >= 1)` = rooms with access level ≥ 1.\n\n**6b.** Two-level aggregation: CTE aggregates per space unit (name, total rooms, access-level-≥1 rooms); outer query filters `al_count > (SELECT AVG(al_count) FROM cte)` — the average of per-space-unit counts, unrounded. Inner join means only space units with room records are considered (consistent with all 5 precedents).\n\n**6c.** *Data*: ZPM_ROOMS_LOAD is the only room table holding both ACCESS_LEVEL and a space-unit link; SPACE_DETAIL lacks ACCESS_LEVEL; FCLT_ROOMS lacks space-unit keys. *Semantic network*: column_concept_access_level decision guide explicitly routes \"room access level analysis … with space unit linkage\" to ZPM_ROOMS_LOAD; concept_room_space_inventory agrees; dw_2616 establishes the above-average two-level pattern. *Business*: 5 existing metrics count access-level rooms per space unit exactly via `SPACE_UNIT.SPACE_UNIT_CODE = ZPM_ROOMS_LOAD.SPACE_UNIT_CODE` with `COUNT(*)` grouped by space unit name.\n\n**6d.** Alternative: SPACE_UNIT2 for the name — rejected because it is a documented strict subset (139 vs 150 units), risking dropped space units. No tie-breaker needed.\n\n",
